  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>Design and ship one or more Claude Code skills that help an agent draft Agda code <html:em>iteratively</html:em>, using the Agda typechecker as the primary oracle instead of reading through the codebase until the context window is exhausted. Any pre-existing Agda skill is unused and should be treated as deprecated — replace rather than extend it.</html:p>
    <html:p><html:strong>Core iteration loop.</html:strong> The skill should enforce a hole-at-a-time workflow:</html:p>
    <html:ul><html:li>Query the types and contexts of all open holes in the target file up front, so the agent has a work plan before editing.</html:li>
  <html:li>For each hole, make <html:em>one</html:em> modular change, then typecheck. Do not advance to the next hole until the current edit typechecks cleanly <html:em>and</html:em> no unsolved metas (yellow) remain — yellow is failure, not success.</html:li>
  <html:li>When stuck, prefer re-introducing <html:code><![CDATA[{!   !}]]></html:code> over committing a guess. Let the compiler narrow the goal.</html:li></html:ul>
    <html:p><html:strong>Runtime is a signal, not just a cost.</html:strong> Every typecheck and every <html:code>agda --interaction</html:code> command runs under a wall-clock budget:</html:p>
    <html:ul><html:li><html:strong>Hard cap around 20s</html:strong> per check or interaction — if Agda hasn't responded, treat the attempt as failed and abandon that route. Do not wait longer hoping it resolves.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:strong>Anything over ~5s is a warning sign</html:strong> for the vast majority of goals. Slow elaboration usually means the agent is fighting the typechecker: wrong lemma driving divergent unification, universe mismatch blowing up constraints, over-concrete instantiation, or reduction that should have been <html:code>abstract</html:code> / <html:code>opaque</html:code>. Treat the slowness as evidence the approach is wrong, not as something to wait out.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:strong>Prune aggressively on slow feedback.</html:strong> When a candidate term makes the typechecker crawl, back it out and try a structurally different approach (different lemma, different case-split order, more holes) rather than tweaking details of the slow term.</html:li>
  <html:li>Some files legitimately take longer (large HITs, heavy transports). Allow a per-project override, but default to the tight budget.</html:li></html:ul>
    <html:p><html:strong>Use the compiler as an oracle, not the codebase.</html:strong> Wrap <html:code>agda --interaction</html:code> (or <html:code>--interaction-json</html:code>) in a bash script so the agent can issue <html:code>Cmd_goal_type_context_infer</html:code>, <html:code>Cmd_refine_or_intro</html:code>, <html:code>Cmd_make_case</html:code>, <html:code>Cmd_auto_maybe_all</html:code>, and <html:code>Cmd_compute</html:code> programmatically. These narrowing tactics produce correct-by-construction skeletons and are far higher density than reading source.</html:p>
    <html:p><html:strong>Context-management rules</html:strong> (the failure mode to prevent: agent reads through stdlib / cubical until the window fills, compacts, then repeats without writing code):</html:p>
    <html:ul><html:li><html:strong>Prefer grep over Read</html:strong> for lemma discovery. Type signatures are one-liners; whole-file reads are almost never necessary.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:strong>Delegate library search to a subagent</html:strong> with a tight prompt ("find functions in <html:code>Cubical.Foundations.*</html:code> whose return type matches <html:code>isEquiv _</html:code>") so exploration fills the subagent's context, not the main one.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:strong>Exhaust the typechecker first.</html:strong> Before reading any file, the agent must have run goal/context inspection, <html:code>auto</html:code>, <html:code>refine</html:code>, and case-split on the current hole.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:strong>Budget exploration explicitly</html:strong>: at most N files read before the next compile attempt. When the budget is hit, write <html:em>something</html:em> so the typechecker's error narrows the search further.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:strong>Scratch-file discovered lemmas</html:strong> (<html:code>Module.foo : T → U</html:code>) so post-compaction the agent doesn't re-discover the same results.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:strong>Fail loudly.</html:strong> After K consecutive edits without a green typecheck, surface the blockage to the user rather than spiraling.</html:li></html:ul>
    <html:p>
      <html:strong>Open questions before implementation.</html:strong>
    </html:p>
    <html:ul><html:li>Single monolithic skill vs. several small ones (e.g. <html:code>agda-fill-hole</html:code>, <html:code>agda-search-lemma</html:code>, <html:code>agda-check</html:code>)?</html:li>
  <html:li>Does the skill need project-aware setup (locate <html:code>.agda-lib</html:code> / <html:code>Cubical</html:code> / <html:code>agda-stdlib</html:code> roots) or can it assume the user's environment is already configured?</html:li>
  <html:li>How should the skill handle Agsy timeouts and partial results from <html:code>auto</html:code>?</html:li>
  <html:li>Cubical vs. vanilla Agda — same skill, or separate flavors for <html:code>--cubical</html:code> pragmas and HITs?</html:li></html:ul>
    <html:p><html:strong>Next steps.</html:strong> Prototype the bash wrapper around <html:code>agda --interaction-json</html:code>, sketch the skill's <html:code>SKILL.md</html:code> frontmatter and trigger conditions, then dogfood on an open hole in <fr:link href="/sss-009X/" title="Double Category Theory in Cubical Categorical Logic" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-009X/" display-uri="sss-009X" type="local">Cubical.Categories.Double.Instances.Displayed</fr:link> or similar WIP Agda work.</html:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
